<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Foscari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1373–1457</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Foscari was Doge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1423 to 1457, the longest reign of any doge. He led the republic into long wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> that won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> and Bergamo and carried Venice's mainland frontier to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a>. In 1457, after the exile of his son Jacopo, the Council of Ten forced him to abdicate, and he died a week later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-foscari.jpg" alt="Francesco Foscari" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Portrait_of_the_Venecian_doge_Francesco_Foscari_by_Lazzaro_Bastiani_-_Correr_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
